function apply(){state=defaultState();view=defaultView();blocked=false;shared=false;if(location.hash.startsWith('#scenario=')){const r=restore(location.hash.slice(10));if(r.state){state=r.state;view=r.view;shared=true;}else{blocked=true;$('scenario-message').textContent=r.error;}}$('scenario-warning').hidden=!blocked;sync();render();}
window.addEventListener('hashchange',()=>{if(data&&(location.hash.startsWith('#scenario=')||shared||blocked))apply();});window.addEventListener('resize',()=>{clearTimeout(resizeTimer);resizeTimer=setTimeout(()=>{if(points)renderChart();},100);});document.addEventListener('click',e=>{const a=e.target.closest?.('a[href^="#"]');if(!a)return;const target=$(a.getAttribute('href').slice(1));if(!target)return;e.preventDefault();if(target.tagName==='DETAILS')target.open=true;target.scrollIntoView({block:'start'});const focus=target.querySelector('summary')||target;focus.focus({preventScroll:true});});
function methods(){$('methods-content').innerHTML=`<h3>Payment model</h3><p>These are modeled national Medicare office E/M allowed amounts, including the patient’s 20% share, after Medicare sequestration. They exclude geographic adjustments, collections, MIPS and separate APM incentives. Practice-cost growth is reflected through the MEI adjustment, not subtracted as an expense.</p><h3>Sequestration</h3><p>Since April 1, 2013, Medicare’s payment has been cut 2% after coinsurance and deductible, so the cut is 2% of Medicare’s 80% share: 1.6% of the allowed amount. It was suspended from May 1, 2020 through March 31, 2022, was 1% from April through June 2022, and has been 2% since July 1, 2022 (scheduled through fiscal year 2032). Partial years use day-weighted rates: 2013 1.51%, 2020 0.66%, 2022 1.26%. Deductibles, which further reduce Medicare’s share for some claims, are not modeled. AWVs, TCM and procedures are outside this visit basket. The same code may represent different documentation or clinical work across years.</p><p>The model holds visits, working days, visit mix and complexity uptake constant. Each year uses its applicable RVUs and conversion factor. Claim lines are rounded to cents before calendar-day weighting across effective pricing periods.</p><p>The illustrative default mix is 95% established and 5% new-patient visits (440 visits a month at the default volume), scaled to your selected volume.</p><p>The 2026 → 2027 breakdown first reprices 2026 RVUs, including G2211, at the 2026 conversion factor without its one-time 2.5% statutory increase, which applied only to 2026 (CY 2027 final rule, Tables D-B1 and D-B2). It then applies the 2027 update and budget-neutrality adjustment, swaps in 2027 RVUs (revaluation), and replaces G2211 with B1/B2 (complexity coding). The four parts add up to the total change. The 2026 baseline uses the January 2026 RVU file.</p><h3>APCM</h3><p>Advanced Primary Care Management codes G0556–G0558 are billable from January 1, 2025, once per patient per month. The model adds your monthly APCM patients at each level, priced at that year’s RVUs and conversion factor (QP or non-QP from 2026), with the same 20% patient share and sequestration as visits. Defaults to none. Add-ons such as the 2026 behavioral-health codes are not modeled.</p><h3>Complexity add-on: G2211, then B1/B2</h3><p>2024–2026: G2211 is a fixed add-on with its own RVUs (0.52 total RVUs in 2026), paying the same amount at every visit level.</p><p>2027: G2211 is replaced by modifiers on the base visit. The CY 2027 final rule sets B1 at “16 percent of the total RVUs for the E/M service on the claim”, and B2 at 32 percent. B2 is limited to clinicians in Medicare Shared Savings Program or LEAD ACOs. Each modifier amount is the visit priced at 116% or 132%, minus the base fee, both rounded to cents.</p><p>The 2027 track questions set two things. MSSP or LEAD participation sets B1 vs B2; the final rule limits B2 to MSSP ACOs (all tracks, including ACO PC Flex) and LEAD, not other Advanced APMs. QP status applies two years later: the 2024 performance year sets the 2026 conversion factor and 2025 sets 2027. The model uses one QP answer for both years, so a change in QP status between 2024 and 2025, or a QP outside an APM in 2027, is not modeled. The complexity percentage applies to all office visits, new and established, and is illustrative.</p><h3>Inflation and conversion factor</h3><p>Adjusted monthly payment = that year's nominal monthly payment × the 2027 index ÷ that year's index.  CF is the year’s dollars per RVU, not the payment for a complete visit.</p><p>CPI uses BLS annual averages through 2025. The MEI series uses CMS’s published annual update chain, including productivity adjustments, rather than realized practice-expense inflation. 2027 fees are scheduled future payments.</p><h3>Projected index assumptions</h3><p>The inflation adjustments use forecasts, not observed full-year inflation, for 2026 and 2027. CPI growth assumptions are 2.924% for 2026 and 2.547% for 2027, from CBO’s February 2026 annual-average projection. The CMS MEI update assumptions are 2.7% and 2.6%, respectively, including productivity adjustments.</p><p><a href="https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62105" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CBO projection</a> · <a href="https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2026-22743.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS 2027 MEI assumption</a></p><p>The 2026 → 2027 panel, the comparison table and the CSV use 2027 dollars. The chart uses the dollars of its end year, so with the default range it is 2027 dollars, and with 2001–2015 it is 2015 dollars. In the end year the adjustment factor is that year’s index divided by itself, 1, which is why all three lines meet there.</p><h3>Historical pricing</h3><p>1992–1995 are national fee-schedule references excluding locality-specific charge-transition blending. Transitional practice-expense values and separate work adjustments are applied where required. The 1997–1998 work product retains precision; 2007–2008 adjusted work is rounded to two decimals before adding practice-expense and malpractice values. Annual prices use all covered effective periods.</p><h3>Data and sharing</h3><p>Dataset ${esc(data.version)}, updated ${esc(data.asOf)}, uses the validated source data. A shared link preserves the practice inputs and four comparison years. Older links fill missing years with defaults. </p><p><a href="data/schedule-${esc(data.version)}.json">Download source data</a></p>`;}
(async()=>{try{const [r,a]=await Promise.all([fetch('./data/schedule-'+DATA_VERSION+'.json'),fetch('./data/apcm-'+APCM_DATA_VERSION+'.json')]);if(!r.ok||!a.ok)throw Error('Schedule unavailable');data=await r.json();data.apcm=await a.json();if(data.version!==DATA_VERSION||data.apcm.version!==APCM_DATA_VERSION)throw Error('Unexpected data version');methods();apply();}catch(e){$('error').hidden=false;$('error').textContent='Could not load payment data: '+e.message;for(const id of ['share','export'])$(id).disabled=true;}})();
